[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked **known**: one issue, one fix queued. Read the reports, not
their subject lines, and it holds three problems. The queued parser fix closes only one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one bug. Three problems, three tickets. Fixing the parser leaves the other two open.
</Callout>

## What the reports show

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first. Day-first tenants get day and
  month swapped: "7 April went out on 4 July". This is the fix that is queued.
- **`hint`**: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, and reads the
  date the tenant's way, day first. People type what the hint tells them, so form bookings at
  booking-v3 tenants swap even after the parser is fixed. A different bug, in the form.
- **`shift`**: the date moves by one day, not by a swap. "Tuesday 12 May, calendar says Monday 11
  May." "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday, client in Toronto got Monday." Time zones, not date
  order. The desk saw these (`desk-notes.json`) and still filed them under `parser`.

## Why it merged

The desk merges on subject line alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
Twelve subject lines ("Wrong day on the invite", "Date is wrong", …) cover all three problems,
so every report landed in #4471. The intake tag did not help either: it guesses from the
reporter's browser, and the table shows how often it disagrees with the tenant.

## Since April

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## What changes

- #4471 stays for `parser`. `hint` and `shift` are tracked in their own tickets (table above).
- Do not close the `hint` or `shift` tickets when the parser fix ships.
- Count a report once: the export repeats some reports by id.

